❯ What about doing items 1–3, 6, 9, 11 that are are API-breaking first. I love to do hardest things first. What is your take on that? Will I have enough tokens to do that ?                                                             

● Good instinct, but let me give you an honest engineering assessment before we dive in.

  The 6 items aren't equal in complexity or context cost:

  ┌──────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Item │                                               What it actually means                                                │             Lines touched             │                  Risk                  │
  ├──────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ 1+2  │ Extract ~15 helper methods from two huge methods in Module6                                                         │ ~1300 lines in one file               │ Low — pure extraction, no logic change │
  ├──────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ 6    │ Fix Kahn's sort to emit let rec … and for cycles                                                                    │ ~40 lines in Module6                  │ Medium — changes generated WhyML       │
  ├──────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ 9    │ Define PyCSLError subclasses + replace raise SyntaxError / sys.exit in ~10 files                                    │ ~25 files, ~5 lines each              │ Low per file, but tedious              │
  ├──────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ 11   │ Replace 80 flat dataclasses with a typed hierarchy in Module2 — and update every isinstance check in M3, M4, M5, M6 │ 5 files, hundreds of isinstance sites │ High — API break cascades              │
  ├──────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ 3    │ Decompose the 2772-line agent-annotate into a guard pipeline with dependency ordering                               │ 2772-line file, near-full rewrite     │ Very high                              │
  └──────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────┘

  On context: reading + rewriting Module6 (~2500 lines), Module2 + 4 downstream files (~3000 lines), all 25 files for item 9, and agent-annotate.py (2772 lines) in one session would almost certainly exhaust the context window before
  we finish. The conversation compresses, but large file rewrites are expensive.

  My recommendation — do it in this order across two sessions:

  Session 1 (this one):
  - Items 1, 2, 6 together — they're all Module6, one file, three targeted changes
  - Item 11 — Module2 hierarchy + downstream cascade (hardest API change, good to validate early)

  Session 2:
  - Item 9 — PyCSLError across all files (many small edits)
  - Item 3 — agent-annotate full guard pipeline rewrite (deserves its own session, it's a near-rewrite of 2772 lines)

  This way if something breaks after items 1, 2, 6, 11 you can run the test suite before proceeding.

  Want to start with that session 1 scope (items 1, 2, 6, 11)?


